<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hanuman Dhoka</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Hanuman Dhoka Durbar</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Hanuman Dhoka is the old royal palace complex on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kathmandu">Kathmandu</a> Durbar Square in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nepal">Nepal</a>, named after a stone image of the god Hanuman that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pratap-malla">Pratap Malla</a> set up at its gate in 1672. It was the seat of the Malla kings of Kantipur and, after 1768, of the Shah kings of Nepal until the late 19th century. Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kathmandu-valley">Kathmandu Valley</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/world-heritage-site">World Heritage Site</a>, it was damaged in the 2015 earthquake.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hanuman-dhoka.jpg" alt="Hanuman Dhoka" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Chainwit. · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hanuman_Dhoka_Durbar_Square_Kathmandu_(2023)_01.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The palace stands in the Durbar Square of central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kathmandu">Kathmandu</a>; dhoka means door or gate in Nepali. The Hanuman Gate is on the west side of the square. The statue of Hanuman beside it is draped in red cloth under an umbrella, its face smeared with red paste, and on the left a stone Narasimha of 1673 devours the demon Hiranyakashipu. The buildings are grouped around courtyards called chok.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The eastern wing, with ten courtyards, is the oldest part, dated to the mid 16th century; King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pratap-malla">Pratap Malla</a> enlarged the palace in the 17th century with many temples. Mohan Chok, built in 1649, was the residential courtyard of the Malla kings, and a Malla heir had to be born there; its golden waterspout, the Sun Dhara, served the kings' daily ablutions. Mul Chok is dedicated to Taleju Bhawani, the tutelary goddess of the Malla family, whose image is brought to its temple during the Dashain festival.</p><p>In 1768 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prithvi-narayan-shah">Prithvi Narayan Shah</a> added four lookout towers in the south-east, among them the nine-storey Basantapur Tower, whose struts carry erotic carvings. The buildings around Nasal Chok, named after an image of the dancing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiva">Shiva</a>, were built by the Rana rulers, and the Maha Vishnu Temple there fell in the 1934 Nepal-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bihar">Bihar</a> earthquake. The royal family lived in the palace until 1886 and then moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/narayanhiti-palace">Narayanhiti Palace</a>, but Birendra was crowned king in Nasal Chok in 1975.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The palace houses the Hanuman Dhoka Palace Museum, run by the Nepalese government, with sections on the Shah dynasty, which ruled until 2006, the Tribhuwan Museum and the King Mahendra Memorial Museum. Outside stands a stone drinking fountain inscribed in fifteen languages.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Hanuman Dhoka” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Pratap Malla, in the mid 17th century, was the most celebrated king: a poet and builder who added to the <b>Hanuman Dhoka</b> palace and set up the Rani Pokhari tank.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-kantipur">Kingdom of Kantipur</a></li></ul></section>
